/target

# Assets are ignored by default and let through one at a time, because the reason differs per file.
# Adding a line here is a licensing decision, not tidying, and every file let through must have an
# entry in assets/CREDITS.md saying who made it and under what terms.
#
# What is in: the Quaternius character and animation kits, which their own License.txt places under
# CC0, and the Warthog, which is CC BY and may travel with the credit that is in CREDITS.md.
# What is out: the mounted gun, which is CC BY-**ND** — see CREDITS.md, where the reasoning is.
/assets/*
!/assets/CREDITS.md

# Whole directories, because everything in them came from the same CC0 kit.
!/assets/animations/
!/assets/characters/

# Except the Mixamo soldier, which is a third answer again: licensed for use and explicitly not
# for redistribution, so it stays out however convenient it would be to have it. `tools/setup-assets`
# is how each developer produces their own copy. Its 49 clips live in /assets/anims/, which is
# covered by the blanket rule above and is deliberately not opened.
/assets/characters/swat.glb

# The models directory holds two files under two different licences, so it is opened and then
# closed again around the one that may travel.
!/assets/models/
/assets/models/*
!/assets/models/warthog.glb

# Ground textures: three ambientCG packs under CC0, the same three webgame's own map names, so the
# two games' ground reads as the same place. CC0 is a public-domain dedication, so these travel
# without conditions — the entry in CREDITS.md is decency, not a requirement. Colour maps only: the
# normal maps are six megabytes each and there is nothing to hang them on until the ground mesh
# carries tangents.
!/assets/textures/
/assets/textures/*
!/assets/textures/Grass001_1K-PNG_Color.png
!/assets/textures/Ground048_1K-PNG_Color.png
!/assets/textures/Rock020_1K-PNG_Color.png

# And the detail beside each of them: normal xy, roughness and height folded into one RGBA image by
# `tools/bake_ground_maps`. Derived files, and the only derived files in this directory — but the
# thing they are derived from is the twenty-megabyte pack in `downloads/`, which is not here and is
# not going to be. Ten megabytes to check in against two gigabytes to fetch before the ground looks
# right is not a close call. Same CC0 as the colour maps they were cut from.
!/assets/textures/Grass001_1K-PNG_Packed.png
!/assets/textures/Ground048_1K-PNG_Packed.png
!/assets/textures/Rock020_1K-PNG_Packed.png

# Shaders are the one thing under assets/ that is not an asset. They are this project's own source
# code, written here, and they live in this directory only because that is where Bevy's asset server
# looks — which is also what lets one be edited and reloaded without a rebuild. No licensing
# question, so no per-file decision: the whole directory is in.
!/assets/shaders/

# ambientCG packs, as `tools/fetch-ground-textures` leaves them. Two gigabytes of zip for the
# hundred-odd Ground materials, kept rather than thrown away because the site sells nothing smaller
# than a whole pack: unpacking a normal map later has to be a local decision, not another download.
/downloads/

# Input traces. Written beside the binary by default, one a session, and none of them belongs in
# the history — a recording is evidence about one afternoon, not a fact about the game.
/*.jsonl

# Byte-code from running the Python helpers in tools/
__pycache__/

# Local network settings; copy noob_tube.example.toml to get started.
/noob_tube.toml

# Maps players make while playing. Content rather than source: a map is written by a running
# server, and the built-in one — the map a server starts on when this directory is empty — lives in
# `terrain.rs` instead.
/maps/
